$y = \sqrt{x + \frac{1}{x^2} + 1} = (x + x^{-2} + 1)^{\frac{1}{2}}$
$\therefore \frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{1}{2}(x + x^{-2} + 1)^{-\frac{1}{2}} \times (1 - 2x^{-3}) \quad \{\text{chain rule}\}
= \frac{1 - \frac{2}{x^3}}{2\sqrt{x + \frac{1}{x^2} + 1}}$

When $x = 4$,
$\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{1 - \frac{2}{4^3}}{2\sqrt{4 + \frac{1}{4^2} + 1}}
= \frac{1 - \frac{2}{64}}{2\sqrt{5 + \frac{1}{16}}}
= \frac{\frac{31}{32}}{2\sqrt{\frac{81}{16}}}
= \frac{\frac{31}{32}}{2 \times \frac{9}{4}}
= \frac{31}{2 \times 32} \times \frac{4}{9}
= \frac{31}{144}$

So, the tangent has gradient $\frac{31}{144}$.
